If we invert afterglow, the clean counterpart is foreglow.

Afterglow:
\text{Cause} \rightarrow \boxed{\text{Event}} \rightarrow \text{residual signal}

Foreglow:
\text{precursor signal} \rightarrow \boxed{\text{Event}} \rightarrow \text{Cause realized}

But your reverse-afterglow is subtler. You’re standing after the event and running the residue backward:

\text{Afterglow}
\rightarrow
\text{Event}
\rightarrow
\text{Route}
\rightarrow
\text{Cause}

Keep reversing and eventually you reach something interesting:

\boxed{\text{Afterglow} \rightarrow \text{Beforeglow}}

A beforeglow would be the latent structure that existed before the visible event—the accumulated conditions that made the event possible.

Think:

smoke → fire → fuel arrangement → conditions that allowed ignition.

So there are really three useful objects:

\underbrace{\text{Beforeglow}}_{\text{latent potential}}
\rightarrow
\underbrace{\text{Flash}}_{\text{realization}}
\rightarrow
\underbrace{\text{Afterglow}}_{\text{causal residue}}

And the Pantheon can traverse either direction:

\text{Beforeglow}
\overset{\text{prediction}}{\longrightarrow}
\text{Flash}
\overset{\text{observation}}{\longrightarrow}
\text{Afterglow}

\text{Afterglow}
\overset{\text{reconstruction}}{\longrightarrow}
\text{Flash}
\overset{\text{inference}}{\longrightarrow}
\text{Beforeglow}

That creates a beautiful loop: afterglow becomes training data for recognizing future beforeglows.

You learn what the footprints of success look like afterward, reconstruct what existed immediately beforehand, and eventually learn to recognize those conditions before the success happens.

That’s hindsight being distilled into foresight.